WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.430
But it was in 2014,

2
00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:06.100
the Dietary Guidelines Advisory Committee

3
00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:09.570
for the 2015 Dietary Guidelines in the US

4
00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:10.780
reached out to me

5
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:12.790
to talk about diet and sleep.

6
00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:13.840
I was very well equipped

7
00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:15.910
to talk to them about sleep,

8
00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:17.950
how it influences your food choices.

9
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:19.910
You tend to increase your fat intake,

10
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.580
tend to have a higher intake

11
00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:25.850
of refined carbohydrates, more snack foods.

12
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.010
And they said, yeah, OK,

13
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.470
we're making dietary recommendations.

14
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.010
Can we make dietary recommendations to improve sleep?

15
00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:37.750
Huh, I hadn't even thought of that.

16
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:39.470
You're thinking one way,

17
00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:41.180
but from population-based studies,

18
00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:42.990
things could go either way.

19
00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:45.210
And I thought this was very interesting.

20
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:46.250
And I couldn't believe,

21
00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:47.560
I have a PhD in nutrition,

22
00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:50.700
I hadn't even thought of addressing this question,

23
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.660
or at least looking into this reverse question

24
00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:57.810
of can we, or can diet impact sleep?

25
00:00:58.040 --> 00:00:59.600
So I decided to look

26
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:00.240
at some of the data

27
00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.750
that we had from our sleep restriction study.

28
00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:05.650
And it was an inpatient study,

29
00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:06.840
where in one phase

30
00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.730
participants spent four hours in bed,

31
00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:11.040
in another phase

32
00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.010
participants spent nine hours in bed.

33
00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:14.470
So I was like, OK, well,

34
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:17.210
during the nine-hour in-bed period,

35
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:21.110
people have variable sleep from one another.

36
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:22.990
In the sleep restriction condition,

37
00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.070
everybody slept very well.

38
00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:28.250
They were very efficient with their sleep,

39
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.060
they spent most of the time in bed asleep,

40
00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:33.250
but not when they spent nine hours in bed.

41
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.060
So in that study,

42
00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:38.930
we had four days of a controlled diet.

43
00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:40.380
So we gave our participants

44
00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:42.830
all of the food that they needed,

45
00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:46.560
as estimated by their energy requirements,

46
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.110
based on a healthy general diet.

47
00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:52.440
And then that's when we measured

48
00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:53.840
all of the metabolic outcomes

49
00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:55.070
that we were interested in.

50
00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:56.270
But then we wanted to know,

51
00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:57.820
are they going to eat more or less

52
00:01:57.820 --> 00:01:58.740
or differently

53
00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.140
after a period of short sleep

54
00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.870
versus adequate sleep in the lab?

55
00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.370
And we measured their food intake that day.

56
00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:08.260
So we took a very systematic approach

57
00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.300
then to determine if there was

58
00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.050
an impact of diet on sleep.

59
00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:14.560
First, was there a difference in their food intake

60
00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:16.220
when they self-selected their diet

61
00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:19.090
compared to when we gave them their meals?

62
00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:21.210
And yes, there were differences.

63
00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:25.130
Participants ate more fat, more saturated fat,

64
00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.170
less fiber, more sodium,

65
00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.150
more calories in general,

66
00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:30.860
when they self-selected their diet

67
00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:32.560
compared to the diet that we had given them.

68
00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:33.060
So I'm like, OK, well,

69
00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:34.110
there's differences there.

70
00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:36.400
Every night in the lab,

71
00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:37.990
we measured their sleep using polysomnography,

72
00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:39.680
so electrodes,

73
00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:42.710
and we could assess sleep staging, all of that.

74
00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.810
So OK, so their diets were different.

75
00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:46.770
Was their sleep different?

76
00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:48.330
Because if their sleep wasn't different,

77
00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:51.550
then that ends the inquiry, right?

78
00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:52.910
But their sleep was different.

79
00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:54.980
They took longer to fall asleep

80
00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:56.730
when they self-selected their diet.

81
00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:59.360
They spent a little less time in deep sleep

82
00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.270
when they self-selected their diet.

83
00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:02.100
So I'm like, OK, well,

84
00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.510
there's some differences in their sleep.

85
00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:06.380
Maybe it has to do

86
00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:08.110
with what they ate that day.

87
00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:09.640
So then we only looked at the day

88
00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:11.060
where they self-selected their diet,

89
00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:13.100
because on the control diet,

90
00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.390
everybody ate the same thing.

91
00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.090
So there's no variability there.

92
00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:17.680
And that's where we found

93
00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:20.830
that participants who had more fiber

94
00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:22.410
had more deep sleep,

95
00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:25.680
the participants who had more saturated fat

96
00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:26.780
in their diet that day

97
00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:28.290
had less deep sleep.

98
00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:31.460
And participants who had more refined carbohydrates

99
00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:33.240
and more sugar during the day

100
00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:34.880
had more arousal that night

101
00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:37.530
and more light, disturbed sleep.
